note: from "interp.py, written in Planes -- Route B stage three, build 1: the expression evaluator. Evaluates Planes expressions to values, checked by agreement with interp.py's own eval() through a canonical value-text form (test_interp_in_planes.py). Consumes the AST grammar/parser.planes already produces (A.4); statements, control flow, and effects are builds 2 and 3. Values carry a deriv slot, `nothing` throughout build 1 (A.3) -- provenance is a later build's fill, not a rewrite."

use parser

# ================================================================ value representation
#
# A value is a tagged record: `kind` names the type, and the payload rides
# in `value` (number / text / boolean / nothing), in `items` (list), or in
# `fields` (record -- a list of { key, value } pairs, because Planes cannot
# build a record with runtime field names, so a record value is never a
# native record but always this tagged list-of-pairs). Every value carries a
# `deriv` slot, `nothing` throughout build 1 (A.3). The tag is what lets the
# renderer and every operator dispatch flatly on a value's type (A.1) without
# a dynamic type probe -- Planes has none that does not itself raise.

to make-number of n:
  give { kind: "number", value: n, deriv: nothing }

to make-text of s:
  give { kind: "text", value: s, deriv: nothing }

to make-boolean of b:
  give { kind: "boolean", value: b, deriv: nothing }

to make-nothing:
  give { kind: "nothing", value: nothing, deriv: nothing }

to make-list of items:
  give { kind: "list", items: items, deriv: nothing }

to make-record of fields:
  give { kind: "record", fields: fields, deriv: nothing }

# ================================================================ the canonical value form (A.5)
#
# Both sides render an evaluated value to this exact text and the test
# compares strings. Numbers render through `text of` -- interp.py's own
# Number.text(), an exact-rational form that never shows a float: 2 / 3 is
# `~0.666666666666`, 0.1 + 0.2 is `0.3`. Strings are escaped and quoted via
# escape-string-literal (the same four escapes interp.py's escaper uses, so
# a quote or newline survives the comparison). Records render their fields in
# stored order. `1`, `"1"`, and `true` render distinctly, so the form names
# the type as well as the value.

to canonical-of-value of v:
  if v.kind == "number":
    give text of v.value
  if v.kind == "text":
    give "\"" + (escape-string-literal of v.value) + "\""
  if v.kind == "boolean":
    if v.value:
      give "true"
    give "false"
  if v.kind == "nothing":
    give "nothing"
  if v.kind == "list":
    give "[" + (canonical-of-items of v.items) + "]"
  if v.kind == "record":
    give "{" + (canonical-of-fields of v.fields) + "}"
  fail "cannot render a value of kind '" + v.kind + "'" as cannot-render

# One pass over the items, comma-joined (the join-comma shape, inlined with
# the per-item render so there is no intermediate list and no per-item
# recursion). An empty list renders its items as "" -- so `[]` and `{}` fall
# out with no special case.
to canonical-of-items of items:
  result = ""
  seen = false
  for each item in items:
    piece = canonical-of-value of item
    if seen:
      result = result + ", " + piece
    else:
      result = piece
      seen = true
  give result

to canonical-of-fields of fields:
  result = ""
  seen = false
  for each f in fields:
    piece = f.key + ": " + (canonical-of-value of f.value)
    if seen:
      result = result + ", " + piece
    else:
      result = piece
      seen = true
  give result

# ================================================================ the environment (A.2)
#
# One flat list of { name, value } bindings, innermost first, first match
# wins -- no scope chain, no nested frames to walk. Entering a function
# prepends its bindings and passes the extended list down (Phase 5); the
# caller keeps its own list unchanged, because lists are immutable, so
# shadowing falls out of scan order. The scan reads each binding's `name`
# and `value` by static field access -- there is no dynamic record lookup.

# The first binding for a name, of any kind: { found, value }. A local
# variable prepended in front shadows one behind it -- first match wins.
to env-find of env, name:
  result = nothing
  found = false
  for each binding in env:
    if not found:
      if binding.name == name:
        result = binding.value
        found = true
  give { found: found, value: result }

# The first *function* binding for a name, skipping any non-function binding
# of the same name -- interp.py keeps functions in their own namespace, so a
# call resolves to the function even when a local variable shares its name.
to function-find of env, name:
  result = nothing
  found = false
  for each binding in env:
    if not found:
      if binding.name == name:
        if binding.value.kind == "function":
          result = binding.value
          found = true
  give { found: found, value: result }

# ================================================================ the status record (A.3)
#
# Every evaluation and execution step takes a status record and returns one.
# All four fields are always present. `normal` carries a value; `give` carries
# a given value up to the function boundary (Rule 2); `fail` carries a
# { tag, detail } error outward until `or fail` catches it (Rule 3). The env
# rides in the record (Rule 4), and a step never reads `value` without first
# checking `status` (Rule 5). These three constructors are the only places the
# record is built, so the shape is defined once.

to normal-of of value, env:
  give { status: "normal", value: value, env: env, error: nothing }

to give-of of value, env:
  give { status: "give", value: value, env: env, error: nothing }

to fail-of of error, env:
  give { status: "fail", value: nothing, env: env, error: error }

# An error is the same { tag, detail } record `or fail as` produces (A.3), so a
# self-hosted failure is not a second kind of error.
to error-of of tag, detail:
  give { tag: tag, detail: detail }

# ================================================================ eval-node (A.1: flat; A.3: status-threaded)
#
# The evaluation step. Dispatch on the parser's node kind with a flat if ...
# give sequence -- never a nested when/else ladder (A.1) -- and thread a status
# record through every sub-evaluation (A.3): a sub-expression that fails or
# gives is passed straight back (Rule 1), and the environment threads left to
# right so an assignment inside an expression-position block is visible after
# it. The value-producing helpers (apply-op, the builtins, equality) stay
# value-returning and raise on a runtime error; each call site catches that raise
# once, at the leaf, and turns it into a fail status -- so propagation is the
# status record's job throughout, and a host exception is never the carrier.
#
# `eval` below is the value-returning adapter build 1's tests and the pipeline
# call; eval-node is the step everything in build 2 threads.

to eval-node of node, env, globals:
  k = node.kind
  if k == "Num":
    give normal-of of (make-number of node.value), env
  if k == "Str":
    give normal-of of (make-text of node.value), env
  if k == "Bool":
    give normal-of of (make-boolean of node.value), env
  if k == "Nothing":
    give normal-of of (make-nothing), env
  if k == "Var":
    give eval-var of node, env, globals
  if k == "BinOp":
    give eval-binop-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "Not":
    give eval-not-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "IsNothing":
    give eval-isnothing-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "ListLit":
    give eval-list-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "RecordLit":
    give eval-record-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "RecordUpdate":
    give eval-record-update-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "ListPlus":
    give eval-list-plus-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "Field":
    give eval-field-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "Round":
    give eval-round-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "Call":
    give eval-call-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "If":
    give eval-if-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "When":
    give eval-when-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "ForEach":
    give eval-foreach-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "OrFail":
    give eval-orfail-node of node, env, globals
  if k == "WriteTo":
    give fail-of of (error-of of "build-3-effect", "writing a file is an effect -- effects are interp.planes build 3, not build 2"), env
  give fail-of of (error-of of "cannot-evaluate", ("cannot evaluate a node of kind '" + k + "'")), env

# A variable reference. A function value auto-calls only when no local variable
# of the same name shadows it (interp.py: `name in funcs and not env.has`) --
# env-find returns the innermost binding, so a shadowing local is returned as a
# value and only a bare function name applies.
to eval-var of node, env, globals:
  binding = env-find of env, node.name
  if not binding.found:
    give fail-of of (error-of of "unknown-name", ("no name '" + node.name + "' here")), env
  if binding.value.kind == "function":
    give apply-function-node of binding.value, [], env, globals, node.name
  give normal-of of binding.value, env

# The value-returning adapter (build 1's `eval`): evaluate a node against an
# environment, giving the value on success and re-raising a fail status as the
# host error it names on failure. Build 1's tests and the pipeline reach the
# evaluator through here; everything in build 2 threads eval-node directly.
to eval of node, env:
  r = eval-node of node, env, (globals-of of env)
  if r.status == "fail":
    give reraise of r.error
  give r.value

# Turn a fail status back into the host exception whose tag it carries, so the
# value-returning adapter refuses exactly as build 1 did. The tags are the
# finite set the evaluator raises; a self-hosted program's own custom `fail`
# tags never reach here -- whole programs run through the status layer, not the
# adapter -- so enumerating the built-in tags is complete. Flat, not nested.
to reraise of err:
  t = err.tag
  d = err.detail
  if t == "unknown-name":
    fail d as unknown-name
  if t == "divided-by-zero":
    fail d as divided-by-zero
  if t == "cannot-combine":
    fail d as cannot-combine
  if t == "cannot-compare":
    fail d as cannot-compare
  if t == "not-a-yes-no":
    fail d as not-a-yes-no
  if t == "not-a-number":
    fail d as not-a-number
  if t == "not-a-record":
    fail d as not-a-record
  if t == "not-a-list":
    fail d as not-a-list
  if t == "not-a-collection":
    fail d as not-a-collection
  if t == "not-text":
    fail d as not-text
  if t == "cannot-render":
    fail d as cannot-render
  if t == "cannot-join":
    fail d as cannot-join
  if t == "empty-list":
    fail d as empty-list
  if t == "cannot-evaluate":
    fail d as cannot-evaluate
  if t == "unknown-operator":
    fail d as unknown-operator
  if t == "unknown-builtin":
    fail d as unknown-builtin
  if t == "wrong-arity":
    fail d as wrong-arity
  if t == "unknown-function":
    fail d as unknown-function
  if t == "build-2-statements":
    fail d as build-2-statements
  if t == "build-3-effect":
    fail d as build-3-effect
  fail d as interp-error

# ================================================================ source -> node (consumes parser.planes, A.4)
#
# tokenize (lexer.planes) then parse-expr (parser.planes) -- the same AST
# records parser.planes already produces, consumed here, not re-derived. The
# three Planes stages meet through this one call.

to node-of-source of src:
  toks = tokenize of src
  known = build-known of toks
  result = parse-expr of toks, known
  give result.node

# ================================================================ operators (Phase 3)
#
# Every precedence level parser.py produces and interp.py evaluates.
# interp.py's coercion and type guards are reproduced exactly, read from
# interp.py, not taken from a specification: `+` does not coerce (it concats
# text with text, appends list to list, adds number to number, and refuses
# anything else); ordering comparison across types errors naming both
# operands; equality across types errors rather than answering `false`.
#
# The arithmetic is metacircular and so exact for free (A.5): to add two
# number values, interp.planes performs `a.value + b.value` in Planes, and
# the host's own exact-rational `+` -- the very operator interp.py would use
# -- produces the answer. 2 / 3 is one third here because it is one third
# there; a float never enters.

to eval-binop-node of node, env, globals:
  op = node.op
  if op == "and":
    give eval-and-node of node, env, globals
  if op == "or":
    give eval-or-node of node, env, globals
  if op == "first":
    give eval-first-node of node, env, globals
  ls = eval-node of node.left, env, globals
  if not (ls.status == "normal"):
    give ls
  rs = eval-node of node.right, ls.env, globals
  if not (rs.status == "normal"):
    give rs
  v = (apply-op of op, ls.value, rs.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, rs.env
  give normal-of of v, rs.env

# `not` / `is nothing`, threaded. `not` requires a yes/no value (interp.py's
# condition()), so a non-boolean operand becomes a fail status at the leaf.
to eval-not-node of node, env, globals:
  v = eval-node of node.expr, env, globals
  if not (v.status == "normal"):
    give v
  c = (as-condition of v.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, v.env
  give normal-of of (make-boolean of (not c)), v.env

to eval-isnothing-node of node, env, globals:
  v = eval-node of node.expr, env, globals
  if not (v.status == "normal"):
    give v
  give normal-of of (make-boolean of (v.value.kind == "nothing")), v.env

# `and` / `or` short-circuit, and -- like interp.py -- require both operands
# to be yes/no values, returning the second operand's condition, not the
# operand itself.
to eval-and-node of node, env, globals:
  ls = eval-node of node.left, env, globals
  if not (ls.status == "normal"):
    give ls
  lc = (as-condition of ls.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, ls.env
  if not lc:
    give normal-of of (make-boolean of false), ls.env
  rs = eval-node of node.right, ls.env, globals
  if not (rs.status == "normal"):
    give rs
  rc = (as-condition of rs.value) or fail as e2:
    give fail-of of e2, rs.env
  give normal-of of (make-boolean of rc), rs.env

to eval-or-node of node, env, globals:
  ls = eval-node of node.left, env, globals
  if not (ls.status == "normal"):
    give ls
  lc = (as-condition of ls.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, ls.env
  if lc:
    give normal-of of (make-boolean of true), ls.env
  rs = eval-node of node.right, ls.env, globals
  if not (rs.status == "normal"):
    give rs
  rc = (as-condition of rs.value) or fail as e2:
    give fail-of of e2, rs.env
  give normal-of of (make-boolean of rc), rs.env

# `first n of x`: a list's first n items, or a text's first n code points.
# interp.py takes `src.value[:int(n.value)]`; the native `first` operator is
# that same prefix, taken on the tagged items for a list and on the raw
# string for text.
to eval-first-node of node, env, globals:
  n = eval-node of node.left, env, globals
  if not (n.status == "normal"):
    give n
  src = eval-node of node.right, n.env, globals
  if not (src.status == "normal"):
    give src
  if src.value.kind == "list":
    r = (first (n.value.value) of src.value.items) or fail as e:
      give fail-of of e, src.env
    give normal-of of (make-list of r), src.env
  if src.value.kind == "text":
    r2 = (first (n.value.value) of src.value.value) or fail as e2:
      give fail-of of e2, src.env
    give normal-of of (make-text of r2), src.env
  give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-collection", ("cannot take the first n of " + (canonical-of-value of src.value))), src.env

to apply-op of op, a, b:
  if op == "+":
    give apply-plus of a, b
  if op == "-":
    give make-number of ((num-of of a) - (num-of of b))
  if op == "*":
    give make-number of ((num-of of a) * (num-of of b))
  if op == "/":
    give make-number of ((num-of of a) / (num-of of b))
  if op == "<":
    give make-boolean of ((cmp-of of a) < (cmp-of of b))
  if op == ">":
    give make-boolean of ((cmp-of of a) > (cmp-of of b))
  if op == "<=":
    give make-boolean of ((cmp-of of a) <= (cmp-of of b))
  if op == ">=":
    give make-boolean of ((cmp-of of a) >= (cmp-of of b))
  if op == "==":
    give make-boolean of (values-equal of a, b)
  if op == "!=":
    give make-boolean of (not (values-equal of a, b))
  if op == "in":
    give make-boolean of (member-of of a, b)
  fail "unknown operator '" + op + "'" as unknown-operator

# `+` is the one operator that reads the operand kinds, because its result
# kind depends on them (text, list, or number). Everything else is one arm.
to apply-plus of a, b:
  if a.kind == "number" and b.kind == "number":
    give make-number of (a.value + b.value)
  if a.kind == "text" and b.kind == "text":
    give make-text of (a.value + b.value)
  if a.kind == "list" and b.kind == "list":
    give make-list of (a.items + b.items)
  fail "cannot combine " + (canonical-of-value of a) + " with " + (canonical-of-value of b) + " using +" as cannot-combine

# The raw number under a number value, or interp.py's not-a-number refusal.
# `-`, `*`, `/` then use the host's own exact arithmetic on the raw numbers,
# so divide-by-zero and the exact-rational result are reproduced by
# construction rather than reimplemented.
to num-of of v:
  if v.kind == "number":
    give v.value
  fail "cannot do arithmetic on " + (canonical-of-value of v) as not-a-number

# The raw scalar an ordering comparison reads. A cross-type comparison is
# left to the host's own guarded `<` (via the raw values), which refuses it
# naming both operands -- interp.py's rule, not restated here.
to cmp-of of v:
  if v.kind == "number":
    give v.value
  if v.kind == "text":
    give v.value
  if v.kind == "boolean":
    give v.value
  fail "cannot order " + (canonical-of-value of v) as cannot-compare

# interp.py's condition(): a yes/no value and nothing else.
to as-condition of v:
  if v.kind == "boolean":
    give v.value
  fail "a condition needs a yes/no value, found " + (canonical-of-value of v) as not-a-yes-no

# ================================================================ equality and membership
#
# interp.py's equal() is guarded: nothing refuses comparison, a yes/no value
# compares only with a yes/no value, and otherwise the two must be the same
# type. Reproduced structurally here because two value records cannot be
# handed to the host's `==` directly -- their `nothing` deriv slot would
# itself trip the nothing-refusal.

to values-equal of a, b:
  if a.kind == "nothing":
    fail "nothing cannot be compared with ==" as cannot-compare
  if b.kind == "nothing":
    fail "nothing cannot be compared with ==" as cannot-compare
  if a.kind == "number" and b.kind == "number":
    give a.value == b.value
  a-bool = a.kind == "boolean"
  b-bool = b.kind == "boolean"
  if a-bool and not b-bool:
    fail "cannot compare a yes/no value with " + (canonical-of-value of b) as cannot-compare
  if b-bool and not a-bool:
    fail "cannot compare " + (canonical-of-value of a) + " with a yes/no value" as cannot-compare
  if a-bool and b-bool:
    give a.value == b.value
  if not (a.kind == b.kind):
    fail "cannot compare " + (canonical-of-value of a) + " with " + (canonical-of-value of b) as cannot-compare
  if a.kind == "text":
    give a.value == b.value
  if a.kind == "list":
    give lists-equal of a.items, b.items
  if a.kind == "record":
    give records-equal of a.fields, b.fields
  fail "cannot compare values of kind '" + a.kind + "'" as cannot-compare

# Different lengths are unequal; same length compares element by element,
# and a cross-type element mismatch raises through values-equal exactly as
# interp.py's nested equal() does.
to lists-equal of xs, ys:
  if not ((count of xs) == (count of ys)):
    give false
  give lists-equal-walk of xs, ys

to lists-equal-walk of xs, ys:
  if (count of xs) == 0:
    give true
  x = first-item of xs
  y = first-item of ys
  if not (values-equal of x, y):
    give false
  give lists-equal-walk of (rest of xs), (rest of ys)

# Records with different fields refuse comparison (interp.py: set(a) !=
# set(b) raises); same field set compares field by field.
to records-equal of af, bf:
  if not ((count of af) == (count of bf)):
    fail "records have different fields" as cannot-compare
  give records-equal-walk of af, bf

to records-equal-walk of af, bf:
  if (count of af) == 0:
    give true
  f = first-item of af
  match = field-in of bf, f.key
  if not match.found:
    fail "records have different fields" as cannot-compare
  if not (values-equal of f.value, match.value):
    give false
  give records-equal-walk of (rest of af), bf

# Find a field by name in a fields list: { found, value }. A list scan on
# each pair's static `key` -- not a dynamic record lookup.
to field-in of fields, name:
  result = nothing
  found = false
  for each f in fields:
    if not found:
      if f.key == name:
        result = f.value
        found = true
  give { found: found, value: result }

# `in`: list membership by lenient equality (interp.py's `a in b` is Python's
# `in`, which uses `==` and is false across types rather than raising), or
# text substring. raw-eq is values-equal made lenient: a kind mismatch, or a
# comparison values-equal would refuse, is simply false.
to raw-eq of a, b:
  if not (a.kind == b.kind):
    give false
  give (values-equal of a, b) or fail as err:
    give false

to member-of of a, b:
  if b.kind == "list":
    found = false
    for each item in b.items:
      if not found:
        if raw-eq of a, item:
          found = true
    give found
  if b.kind == "text":
    if a.kind == "text":
      give (a.value) in (b.value)
    fail "cannot look for " + (canonical-of-value of a) + " in text" as cannot-combine
  fail "cannot check membership in " + (canonical-of-value of b) as unknown-operator

# ================================================================ lists, records, field access (Phase 4)

# A list literal, threaded: each item evaluated left to right against the
# threaded environment, and the first non-normal status short-circuits the rest
# (Rule 1, expressed as an accumulator guard so the loop stays one `for each`
# pass and takes no per-item recursion frame).
to eval-list-node of node, env, globals:
  items = []
  state = normal-of of nothing, env
  for each n in node.items:
    if state.status == "normal":
      r = eval-node of n, state.env, globals
      if r.status == "normal":
        items = items plus r.value
      state = r
  if not (state.status == "normal"):
    give state
  give normal-of of (make-list of items), state.env

# interp.py builds a record as a Python dict, so a repeated field keeps its
# first position and its last value. eval-record-node reproduces that by
# upserting: a new key appends, a repeated key replaces in place -- threading
# status as the list literal does.
to eval-record-node of node, env, globals:
  fields = []
  state = normal-of of nothing, env
  for each fn in node.fields:
    if state.status == "normal":
      r = eval-node of fn.value, state.env, globals
      if r.status == "normal":
        fields = upsert-field of fields, fn.key, r.value
      state = r
  if not (state.status == "normal"):
    give state
  give normal-of of (make-record of fields), state.env

to upsert-field of fields, key, val:
  if (field-in of fields, key).found:
    give replace-field of fields, key, val
  give fields plus { key: key, value: val }

to replace-field of fields, key, val:
  result = []
  for each f in fields:
    if f.key == key:
      result = result plus { key: key, value: val }
    else:
      result = result plus f
  give result

# `base with f: v, ...` -- a new record differing in the named fields, the
# base untouched. interp.py's {**base, **updates}: existing keys update in
# place, new keys append in update order.
to eval-record-update-node of node, env, globals:
  base = eval-node of node.base, env, globals
  if not (base.status == "normal"):
    give base
  if not (base.value.kind == "record"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-record", ("cannot update " + (canonical-of-value of base.value) + " with with")), base.env
  fields = base.value.fields
  state = base
  for each fn in node.fields:
    if state.status == "normal":
      r = eval-node of fn.value, state.env, globals
      if r.status == "normal":
        fields = upsert-field of fields, fn.key, r.value
      state = r
  if not (state.status == "normal"):
    give state
  give normal-of of (make-record of fields), state.env

# `base plus item` -- a new list with item appended, the base untouched.
to eval-list-plus-node of node, env, globals:
  base = eval-node of node.base, env, globals
  if not (base.status == "normal"):
    give base
  if not (base.value.kind == "list"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-list", ("cannot append to " + (canonical-of-value of base.value) + " with plus")), base.env
  item = eval-node of node.item, base.env, globals
  if not (item.status == "normal"):
    give item
  give normal-of of (make-list of (base.value.items plus item.value)), item.env

# Field access. interp.py reads obj.value.get(name) -- a missing field is
# `nothing`, not an error; only a non-record base is refused.
to eval-field-node of node, env, globals:
  obj = eval-node of node.obj, env, globals
  if not (obj.status == "normal"):
    give obj
  if not (obj.value.kind == "record"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-record", ("cannot read ." + node.name + " from " + (canonical-of-value of obj.value))), obj.env
  match = field-in of obj.value.fields, node.name
  if match.found:
    give normal-of of match.value, obj.env
  give normal-of of (make-nothing), obj.env

# `round x to n places` -- reproduced metacircularly: the host's own round,
# on the raw number, is interp.py's Number.round_to. Only a non-number base
# is refused here; the host handles the places argument.
to eval-round-node of node, env, globals:
  v = eval-node of node.value, env, globals
  if not (v.status == "normal"):
    give v
  p = eval-node of node.places-value, v.env, globals
  if not (p.status == "normal"):
    give p
  if not (v.value.kind == "number"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-number", ("cannot round " + (canonical-of-value of v.value))), p.env
  r = (round (v.value.value) to (p.value.value) places) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, p.env
  give normal-of of (make-number of r), p.env

# ================================================================ builtins (Phase 4)
#
# Ten builtins exist; the eight pure ones are expressions this build
# evaluates -- count, text, lower, upper, whole, normalize, join, rest. The
# two effect-bearing ones, ask and read, are builds 3: they reach a case that
# fails naming build 3 (A.6), never a silent stub. A builtin is an ordinary
# call in the parser (never a Builtin node), routed here by name.

to is-builtin-name of name:
  found = false
  for each b in builtins:
    if not found:
      if b == name:
        found = true
  give found

to eval-call-node of node, env, globals:
  name = node.name
  args = node.args
  # A user function wins over a builtin of the same name (interp.py: a name in
  # funcs is never routed to a builtin) -- the shadowing build-known already
  # relies on, here on the value environment.
  fn = function-find of env, name
  if fn.found:
    give apply-function-node of fn.value, args, env, globals, name
  if is-builtin-name of name:
    if not ((count of args) == 1):
      give fail-of of (error-of of "wrong-arity", ("'" + name + "' takes 1 value, given " + (text of (count of args)))), env
    a = eval-node of (first-item of args), env, globals
    if not (a.status == "normal"):
      give a
    # eval-builtin is value-returning; ask/read raise the build-3 refusal, so
    # the leaf catch turns any builtin failure into a fail status.
    v = (eval-builtin of name, a.value) or fail as e:
      give fail-of of e, a.env
    give normal-of of v, a.env
  give fail-of of (error-of of "unknown-function", ("no function named '" + name + "'")), env

to eval-builtin of name, arg:
  if name == "count":
    give builtin-count of arg
  if name == "text":
    give builtin-text of arg
  if name == "lower":
    give make-text of (lower of (text-form-of of arg))
  if name == "upper":
    give make-text of (upper of (text-form-of of arg))
  if name == "normalize":
    give make-text of (normalize of (text-form-of of arg))
  if name == "whole":
    give builtin-whole of arg
  if name == "join":
    give builtin-join of arg
  if name == "rest":
    give builtin-rest of arg
  if name == "ask":
    fail "ask is an effect -- effects are interp.planes build 3, not build 1" as build-3-effect
  if name == "read":
    fail "read is an effect -- effects are interp.planes build 3, not build 1" as build-3-effect
  fail "unknown builtin '" + name + "'" as unknown-builtin

# count of a list is its item count, of text its code-point count, of a
# record its field count -- interp.py's len(arg.value) over each shape.
to builtin-count of arg:
  if arg.kind == "list":
    give make-number of (count of arg.items)
  if arg.kind == "text":
    give make-number of (count of arg.value)
  if arg.kind == "record":
    give make-number of (count of arg.fields)
  fail "cannot count " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as not-a-collection

# `text of` is interp.py's fmt: a value's plain display form, distinct from
# the canonical form (a list is "[n items]", a record is "{record}", a string
# is itself with no quotes). Used everywhere a value is spliced into a
# message, so it is exact here.
to builtin-text of arg:
  if arg.kind == "number":
    give make-text of (text of arg.value)
  if arg.kind == "text":
    give make-text of arg.value
  if arg.kind == "boolean":
    if arg.value:
      give make-text of "true"
    give make-text of "false"
  if arg.kind == "nothing":
    give make-text of "nothing"
  if arg.kind == "list":
    give make-text of ("[" + (text of (count of arg.items)) + " items]")
  if arg.kind == "record":
    give make-text of "{record}"
  fail "cannot render " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as cannot-render

# The string form lower / upper / normalize operate on. interp.py coerces via
# str(); reproduced for the sensible inputs (text and number). A non-text,
# non-number argument is refused rather than leaking a host repr.
to text-form-of of v:
  if v.kind == "text":
    give v.value
  if v.kind == "number":
    give text of v.value
  fail "cannot take the text of " + (canonical-of-value of v) as not-text

to builtin-whole of arg:
  if arg.kind == "number":
    give make-number of (whole of arg.value)
  fail "cannot take the whole part of " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as not-a-number

# Fold a list of text into one string. A non-list, or any non-text element,
# is refused naming the fix -- the same refusal interp.py's join makes, not a
# silent stringify. An empty list joins to the empty string.
to builtin-join of arg:
  if not (arg.kind == "list"):
    fail "cannot join " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as cannot-join
  strings = []
  for each item in arg.items:
    if not (item.kind == "text"):
      fail "join needs a list of text, found " + (canonical-of-value of item) as cannot-join
    strings = strings plus item.value
  give make-text of (join of strings)

# The list without its first element. Text wants `first n of`, not rest; the
# rest of an empty list is an error, not a silent empty list.
to builtin-rest of arg:
  if arg.kind == "text":
    fail "cannot take the rest of text " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as not-a-list
  if not (arg.kind == "list"):
    fail "cannot take the rest of " + (canonical-of-value of arg) as not-a-list
  if (count of arg.items) == 0:
    fail "cannot take the rest of an empty list" as empty-list
  give make-list of (rest of arg.items)

# ================================================================ lexical scoping (A.1)
#
# The environment a function is applied against is its *definition*
# environment, not the caller's. interp.py gives each call a fresh Env(fn.env),
# and every function in a program is defined at the top level (hoist folds even
# nested defs into one flat funcs namespace), so a function's definition
# environment is the globals: the function values, and nothing of the caller's
# locals. globals-of keeps exactly the function bindings of an environment and
# drops the rest, so a callee sees every function (mutual recursion) and its
# own parameters, but never a name bound only in its caller -- the divergence a
# dynamic-scoping evaluator produces, which build 1's tests could not detect
# because its bodies had no locals to leak.
#
# This is also what stops the environment growing with call depth: with the
# caller's locals dropped, a callee's environment is params + the fixed set of
# function values, the same size at depth 1 and depth 100. There is no dead
# binding to prune because none is ever carried down (A.1: do not implement
# pruning; lexical scoping deletes the problem).
to globals-of of env:
  result = []
  for each binding in env:
    if binding.value.kind == "function":
      result = result plus binding
  give result

# ================================================================ function application (Phase 5)
#
# A function is a tagged value living in the environment, applied against its
# definition environment: its parameters are bound and prepended to the globals
# (globals-of the call environment), and that list -- not the caller's -- is
# passed down to the body (A.1, A.2). No stored closure: the globals always
# carry every function value, so recursion and calls between functions resolve
# without a cyclic record, and no caller local ever reaches the callee.
#
# Build 1 applies functions whose body is a single give-expression. A
# multi-statement body, or any body that is not a lone `give`, is control
# flow -- interp.planes build 2 -- and fails naming it, never a wrong stub
# (A.6's honest-failure pattern).

to make-function of funcdef:
  give { kind: "function", name: funcdef.name, params: funcdef.params, body: funcdef.body, deriv: nothing }

to apply-function-node of fn, arg-nodes, call-env, globals, name:
  if not ((count of fn.params) == (count of arg-nodes)):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "wrong-arity", ("'" + name + "' takes " + (text of (count of fn.params)) + " values, given " + (text of (count of arg-nodes)))), call-env
  bound = eval-args of fn.params, arg-nodes, call-env, globals, []
  if not (bound.status == "normal"):
    give bound
  # A.1: the callee runs against its parameters over the globals -- the fixed
  # function set threaded from the entry point, never the caller's locals. The
  # globals are computed once (globals-of the entry environment) and carried
  # unchanged, so a call is an O(1) prepend, not a per-call scan of the env.
  callee-env = bound.value + globals
  give run-body of fn, callee-env, globals, call-env, name

# Evaluate each argument in the calling environment, pairing it with its
# parameter name, threading status so an argument that fails stops the call.
# Structural recursion over the parameter list -- one frame per parameter, and
# functions take few. Returns a normal status whose value is the bindings list.
to eval-args of params, arg-nodes, call-env, globals, acc:
  if (count of params) == 0:
    give normal-of of acc, call-env
  a = first-item of arg-nodes
  r = eval-node of a, call-env, globals
  if not (r.status == "normal"):
    give r
  p = first-item of params
  give eval-args of (rest of params), (rest of arg-nodes), call-env, globals, (acc plus { name: p, value: r.value })

# Run a function body against the callee environment and carry the result out
# to the caller (Rule 2). Build 1 restricts a body to a single give-expression;
# Phase 7 replaces this with a block run through exec-block, at which point a
# `give` anywhere in the body returns the function's value and a fall-through
# returns nothing. Until then a multi-statement body fails naming build 2 --
# now build 2 is here, so Phase 7 lifts the restriction rather than this stub.
to run-body of fn, callee-env, globals, call-env, name:
  if not ((count of fn.body) == 1):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "build-2-statements", ("'" + name + "' has a multi-statement body -- statements are interp.planes build 2 (Phase 7)")), call-env
  stmt = first-item of fn.body
  if not (stmt.kind == "Give"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "build-2-statements", ("'" + name + "' body is not a single give-expression -- control flow is interp.planes build 2 (Phase 7)")), call-env
  r = eval-node of stmt.expr, callee-env, globals
  if not (r.status == "normal"):
    give r
  give normal-of of r.value, call-env

# ================================================================ control flow as expressions (Phases 3, 5, 6)
#
# If / when / for each / or fail are expressions in interp.py's eval as well as
# statements in exec_stmt -- the same block-running logic in both. eval-node
# dispatches to these; each is filled in its phase. Until then a use reaches a
# fail status naming the phase, never a wrong value.

# `if cond: then else: els` (Phase 3). interp.py runs the chosen block in the
# SAME environment -- no child scope, so a name a branch binds is visible after
# the if -- and the if's value is the block's last result. Both hold here: the
# block runs from a normal start over the (threaded) current env, and its final
# status is returned directly, so a give or fail inside a branch propagates and
# a binding rides out in the env. The condition must be a yes/no value
# (interp.py's condition()), so a non-boolean is a fail at the leaf.
to eval-if-node of node, env, globals:
  c = eval-node of node.cond, env, globals
  if not (c.status == "normal"):
    give c
  cond = (as-condition of c.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, c.env
  if cond:
    give exec-block of node.then, (normal-of of nothing, c.env), globals
  give exec-block of node.els, (normal-of of nothing, c.env), globals

# `when subject is { pattern }: body else: els` (Phase 3, shape dispatch). The
# subject must be a record; the pattern is a list of entries, each a `match`
# (field: expr -- the field must be present and equal, through the same guarded
# equality `==` uses, so a wrong-shaped field fails rather than silently missing)
# or a `bind` (field -- the field's value bound under its name). A missing field
# is no match. On a match the bindings are prepended to the environment and the
# body runs; otherwise els runs. Like `if`, no child scope and the block's last
# result is the value.
to eval-when-node of node, env, globals:
  subj = eval-node of node.subject, env, globals
  if not (subj.status == "normal"):
    give subj
  if not (subj.value.kind == "record"):
    give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-record", ("cannot match " + (canonical-of-value of subj.value) + " against a shape")), subj.env
  m = match-pattern of node.pattern, subj.value, subj.env, globals, []
  if not (m.status == "normal"):
    give m
  if m.value.matched:
    bound-env = m.value.bindings + subj.env
    give exec-block of node.body, (normal-of of nothing, bound-env), globals
  give exec-block of node.els, (normal-of of nothing, subj.env), globals

# Walk the pattern entries, threading status (a match expression may fail), and
# return { matched, bindings }. A missing field or an unequal match value stops
# with matched false; a bind entry collects the field value under its name.
to match-pattern of entries, subject, env, globals, bindings:
  if (count of entries) == 0:
    give normal-of of { matched: true, bindings: bindings }, env
  entry = first-item of entries
  found = field-in of subject.fields, entry.field
  if not found.found:
    give normal-of of { matched: false, bindings: [] }, env
  if entry.matcher-tag == "match":
    want = eval-node of entry.value, env, globals
    if not (want.status == "normal"):
      give want
    eq = (values-equal of found.value, want.value) or fail as e:
      give fail-of of e, want.env
    if not eq:
      give normal-of of { matched: false, bindings: [] }, want.env
    give match-pattern of (rest of entries), subject, want.env, globals, bindings
  give match-pattern of (rest of entries), subject, env, globals, (bindings plus { name: entry.field, value: found.value })

# `for each v in source where cond: body` (Phase 5), both the statement form and
# the comprehension (is-expr). interp.py runs each item in a fresh child scope
# binding the loop variable, so the variable does not leak and a `let` inside the
# body is per-iteration, while a reassignment of an OUTER variable walks to the
# parent and persists (the accumulator idiom). Both hold here: each item runs
# against [{ var, item }] over the current outer env, and after the item the
# outer env is reconstructed with the body's reassignments applied and the
# per-iteration additions dropped. A string iterates its code points (one-code-
# point strings, the language's rule). Like interp.py, the result is always the
# list of per-item body values -- the comprehension's list, discarded when the
# form is used as a statement, while the env mutations ride out.
#
# The loop is one native `for each` pass, not per-item recursion, so a loop over
# a long collection does not consume the recursion ceiling -- only the body's own
# calls do.
to eval-foreach-node of node, env, globals:
  src = eval-node of node.source, env, globals
  if not (src.status == "normal"):
    give src
  if src.value.kind == "list":
    give run-foreach of node, src.value.items, src.env, globals
  if src.value.kind == "text":
    chars = []
    for each c in src.value.value:
      chars = chars plus (make-text of c)
    give run-foreach of node, chars, src.env, globals
  give fail-of of (error-of of "not-a-collection", ("cannot loop over " + (canonical-of-value of src.value))), src.env

to run-foreach of node, items, start-env, globals:
  outer = start-env
  results = []
  broke = false
  carry = normal-of of nothing, start-env
  for each item in items:
    if not broke:
      st = foreach-item-step of node, item, outer, globals
      if st.broke:
        broke = true
        carry = st.status
      else:
        outer = st.env
        if st.kept:
          results = results plus st.value
  if broke:
    give carry
  give normal-of of (make-list of results), outer

# One item: bind the loop variable over the outer env, test the `where` clause
# (a non-yes/no result fails at the leaf), and run the body (an expression for a
# comprehension, a block for the statement form). Returns whether the loop broke
# (a give or fail short-circuits the rest), whether the item was kept (survived
# `where`), its value, and the next outer env with the body's outer-variable
# reassignments applied.
to foreach-item-step of node, item, outer, globals:
  iter-env = [{ name: node.var, value: item }] + outer
  keep = true
  if not (node.where is nothing):
    wr = eval-node of node.where, iter-env, globals
    if not (wr.status == "normal"):
      give { broke: true, status: wr, kept: false, value: nothing, env: outer }
    kc = (as-condition of wr.value) or fail as e:
      give { broke: true, status: (fail-of of e, iter-env), kept: false, value: nothing, env: outer }
    keep = kc
  if not keep:
    give { broke: false, status: (normal-of of nothing, outer), kept: false, value: nothing, env: outer }
  bs = foreach-body of node, iter-env, globals
  if not (bs.status == "normal"):
    give { broke: true, status: bs, kept: false, value: nothing, env: outer }
  give { broke: false, status: bs, kept: true, value: bs.value, env: (rebind-outer of outer, bs.env) }

to foreach-body of node, iter-env, globals:
  if node.is-expr:
    give eval-node of (first-item of node.body), iter-env, globals
  give exec-block of node.body, (normal-of of nothing, iter-env), globals

# Reconstruct the outer environment after an iteration: each outer name takes
# its current value from the body's environment (so a reassignment persists),
# and the per-iteration additions -- the loop variable and any body-local lets --
# are dropped, keeping the outer env the same size across the loop.
to rebind-outer of outer-env, body-env:
  result = []
  for each ob in outer-env:
    looked = env-find of body-env, ob.name
    if looked.found:
      result = result plus { name: ob.name, value: looked.value }
    else:
      result = result plus ob
  give result

to eval-orfail-node of node, env, globals:
  give fail-of of (error-of of "todo-phase-6", "or fail is Phase 6"), env

# ================================================================ statement execution (Phase 2, A.3)
#
# A block is a list of statements executed in order, threading one status
# record (Rule 4: the environment rides in it). exec-stmt begins by checking
# the incoming status and, if it is not "normal", returns its input unchanged
# without evaluating anything (Rule 1) -- the substitute for early exit, and the
# mechanical heart of the build. run-stmt is only ever reached from the normal
# branch, so it stands on a checked status (Rule 5).

to exec-stmt of stmt, state, globals:
  if not (state.status == "normal"):
    give state
  give run-stmt of stmt, state, globals

to exec-block of stmts, state, globals:
  threaded = state
  for each stmt in stmts:
    threaded = exec-stmt of stmt, threaded, globals
  give threaded

# Dispatch a single statement, flat (A.1). Assignment and give are here; show
# is Phase 4 and fail is Phase 6. A definition, note, use, or rule is a no-op
# during execution -- functions are hoisted before the block runs, an
# annotation is display-only, a use only names a module, and a rule is a
# constraint the checker reads, never an action. Everything else is an
# expression evaluated for its value (and its effect on the threaded env),
# which routes if / when / for each / or fail through eval-node's arms.
to run-stmt of stmt, state, globals:
  k = stmt.kind
  if k == "Assign":
    give exec-assign of stmt, state, globals
  if k == "Give":
    r = eval-node of stmt.expr, state.env, globals
    if not (r.status == "normal"):
      give r
    give give-of of r.value, r.env
  if k == "Show":
    give exec-show of stmt, state, globals
  if k == "Note":
    give state
  if k == "Use":
    give state
  if k == "Rule":
    give state
  if k == "Foreign":
    give state
  if k == "FuncDef":
    give state
  r = eval-node of stmt, state.env, globals
  if not (r.status == "normal"):
    give r
  give normal-of of r.value, r.env

# Assignment (Phase 4). `let` binds a new name in this scope (bind_local:
# prepend, innermost first, A.2). Reassignment (is-let false) rebinds an
# existing name where it lives rather than shadowing it -- interp.py's env.set:
# the first matching binding is replaced in place, so the environment does not
# grow on a rebind and a loop that reassigns an accumulator stays flat; a name
# not yet bound is bound locally. A binding value that fails to evaluate
# propagates the fail status.
to exec-assign of stmt, state, globals:
  r = eval-node of stmt.expr, state.env, globals
  if not (r.status == "normal"):
    give r
  if stmt.is-let:
    give normal-of of r.value, ([{ name: stmt.name, value: r.value }] + r.env)
  give normal-of of r.value, (set-in-env of r.env, stmt.name, r.value)

# Rebind the first binding of `name` in place, or prepend one if `name` is new
# (interp.py's env.set over a single flat scope). One `for each` pass, no growth
# on a rebind.
to set-in-env of env, name, value:
  found = false
  result = []
  for each binding in env:
    if (not found) and (binding.name == name):
      result = result plus { name: name, value: value }
      found = true
    else:
      result = result plus binding
  if found:
    give result
  give [{ name: name, value: value }] + env

# `show x` (Phase 4, the one effect in scope, A.5). interp.py renders the value
# with fmt -- a plain display form, the same one `text of` produces -- and hands
# the text to the host. interp.planes reproduces that by delegating through its
# own `show`: the text form is `builtin-text of the value` (fmt exactly), and
# executing `show` here runs the host's show on the outermost interpreter. No
# effect is invented; the path is the host's. show returns the shown value.
to exec-show of stmt, state, globals:
  r = eval-node of stmt.expr, state.env, globals
  if not (r.status == "normal"):
    give r
  txt = (builtin-text of r.value) or fail as e:
    give fail-of of e, r.env
  show (txt.value)
  give normal-of of r.value, r.env

# ================================================================ whole-program execution (Phase 2 driver; Phase 8 finishes)
#
# Parse a program, hoist its function values into the globals, and run the
# top-level statements as one block threading a status record from a normal
# start. Returns the final status record: its env carries every top-level
# binding, its status is normal (or give/fail if the program ended that way).
# Phase 4 adds show output; Phase 8 drives the corpus through it.

to execute-program of src:
  nodes = program-nodes-of of src
  globals = functions-env-of of src
  start = { status: "normal", value: nothing, env: globals, error: nothing }
  give exec-block of nodes, start, globals

# ================================================================ building a function environment (A.4 pipeline support)
#
# Parse a whole program and turn each top-level definition into a function
# value bound by name. The base environment the pipeline evaluates an
# expression against.

to program-nodes-of of src:
  toks = tokenize of src
  known = build-known of toks
  initial = { out: [], cursor: toks, done: false, tokens: toks, known: known }
  final = run-program of initial, toks
  give final.out

to functions-env-of of src:
  nodes = program-nodes-of of src
  env = []
  for each n in nodes:
    if n.kind == "FuncDef":
      env = env plus { name: n.name, value: (make-function of n) }
  give env

# ================================================================ the pipeline, connected (Phase 6, A.4)
#
# Source text in, canonical value out, entirely in Planes: tokenize
# (lexer.planes) -> parse (parser.planes) -> eval (interp.planes) -> render.
# The three Planes stages meet in this one call; the only Python in the path
# is the host running the outermost interpreter.

to evaluate-source of src:
  give canonical-of-value of (eval of (node-of-source of src), [])

# The same, for an expression evaluated against a program's functions.
to evaluate-with of defs-src, expr-src:
  env = functions-env-of of defs-src
  give canonical-of-value of (eval of (node-of-source of expr-src), env)
